  "query_preview": "..\nLingering showers (with a few embedded lightning strikes) are \naround Southeastern Arizona in two main groupings. One to the\nsouth of Tucson along the International Border and one to the \nnorth of Tucson spanning from Tohono Oodham Nation to the White \nMountains. Some accumulations of up to 0.10 inches of rain can\ncome from these, and should begin to move out of the area later\nin the morning.\n\nThe high pressure continues to be in a favorable position in\ncentral/northern Arizona, keeping the easterly flow aloft\nbringing more chances for this afternoon and evening \nthunderstorms to Southeastern Arizona. Lingering overnight \nactivity and the needed time for the atmosphere to recover, \nleading to a later start time for storm initiation this \nafternoon/evening. This is showing up with the CAMs having \ninitiation in the White Mountains around 1 to 2 PM then more \nwidespread initiation 3 to 4 PM. Similar to yesterday, models\nshow storms will begin in the NE then slowly move SW/W over the\ncourse of the afternoon and evening. Tucson will begin to have \nstorm chances beginning after 5/6 PM and moving westward into \nthe evening. Main impacts for storms today follow the \nwind/blowing dust threat area wide, then an added flash flooding\nthreat areas south and east of Tucson along the International \nBorder in southern Cochise and Santa Cruz Counties. The 05/00Z \nHREF once again is showing a large area of 10% probability of 50\nkts. With this, the threat for blowing dust continues with the \nstorm outflows, however with the amount of rainfall that has\nfallen, the confidence for a widespread dust threat lower than\ndays previous, thus no dust products as of now. As for the \nflash flooding component, the 05/00Z HREF is once again \nhighlighting Cochise and Santa Cruz counties with the 3 hour \nprobability matched mean of 1 to 3 inches of rainfall, but\nsimilar to previous days, storm motions will be 15-20kts, so\ntraining storms over the same areas as days previous will be \nthe culprit of flash flooding. \n\nFavorable flow for monsoon activity continues through the week \nwith the upper high drifting north towards the Four\nCorners/Northern Arizona with day to day variability. Forecast \nhigh temperatures will fall to 2-7 degrees above normal, with \nsome variability due to thunderstorm activity and cloud cover.",
